 ## Operating characteristics
-**Time and Memory Complexity:** The method reduces the total number of MLPs running compared to the number of rays $N$ when $N_p > 2$ (for R2) or $N_p > 3$ (for R4, R6) [S1].
+**Time and Memory Complexity:**
+The method reduces training time compared to the original NeRF by parallelizing MLP evaluations. The total number of MLPs running is $N \frac{2}{N_p}$, which is less than $N$ when $N_p > 2$ [S1].
 
-**Measured Performance:** On the Blender dataset with $N_p=4$, it achieves PSNR 30.95, SSIM 0.950, LPIPS 0.060, and T-time 3.65h [S1]. On the LLFF dataset with $N_p=4$, it achieves PSNR 27.35, SSIM 0.861, LPIPS 0.169, and T-time 2.70h [S1]. On the Blender dataset with $N_p=8$, it achieves PSNR 30.02, SSIM 0.940, LPIPS 0.076, and T-time 2.61h [S1]. On the LLFF dataset with $N_p=8$, it achieves PSNR 26.52, SSIM 0.833, LPIPS 0.207, and T-time 2.13h [S1].
+**Measured Performance:**
+*   **Blender Dataset ($N_p=4$):** PSNR 30.95, SSIM 0.950, LPIPS 0.060, Training Time 3.65h [S1].
+*   **LLFF Dataset ($N_p=4$):** PSNR 27.35, SSIM 0.861, LPIPS 0.169, Training Time 2.70h [S1].
+*   **Blender Dataset ($N_p=8$):** PSNR 30.02, SSIM 0.940, LPIPS 0.076, Training Time 2.61h [S1].
+*   **LLFF Dataset ($N_p=8$):** PSNR 26.52, SSIM 0.833, LPIPS 0.207, Training Time 2.13h [S1]
 
-**Scaling:** Increasing $N_p$ from 4 to 8 reduces training time on both datasets (e.g., Blender T-time from 3.65h to 2.61h) while slightly decreasing PSNR (30.95 to 30.02) [S1].
+**Scaling and Trade-offs:**
+MIMO-NeRF-self-R2 achieves performance comparable to MIMO-NeRF-distill while being faster than the original NeRF [S1]. It is one of the configurations that achieves the best or comparable performance in terms of PSNR, SSIM, and LPIPS among the reformulation methods tested, though MIMO-NeRF-self-R1 is generally preferred for the main experiments [S1].
 
-**Strengths and Limitations:** It achieves faster training than the original NeRF (4.70h on Blender, 3.39h on LLFF) and MIMO-NeRF-naive, while maintaining performance comparable to MIMO-NeRF-distill [S1]. Performance is generally lower than the primary MIMO-NeRF-self-R1 variant in most metrics, though R2 is competitive on specific configurations (e.g., $N_p=4$ on Blender and LLFF) [S1].
+## Background
+MIMO-NeRF-self-R2 was introduced in the paper "MIMO-NeRF: Fast Neural Rendering with Multi-input Multi-output Neural Radiance Fields" [S1]. The paper explores various reformulation methods (R1 through R6) to optimize the training speed of NeRF by leveraging multi-input multi-output neural networks. MIMO-NeRF-self-R2 is one of the specific variants analyzed in Table 6 of the source, demonstrating the effect of changing the reformulation method on rendering quality and training time [S1].
 
-**When to Use:** Use MIMO-NeRF-self-R2 when a balance between training speed and rendering quality is needed, specifically when $N_p=4$ is used, as it offers a good trade-off with faster training than NeRF and comparable quality to more complex variants [S1].
